{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XfXjSHJbzPNf4bkQ67pCpa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# UMR guidelines, abridged\n2\t\n3\tA short version of the UMR 0.9 specification (`umr_guidelines.md` in this folder, umr4nlp/umr-guidelines),\n4\tfor annotating Chinese and English at Stage 1 (numbered PropBank roles). It keeps what an annotator needs for\n5\tmost sentences and leaves out the typological material, the fine-grained lattices, the field-linguistics\n6\tsections and most of the examples. Where the full guideline uses the older short names (`AUTH`, `DCT`,\n7\t`FullAff`, `:modstr`, `:ref-person`, `Performance`), this version uses the names of the UMR release data\n8\t(`author`, `document-creation-time`, `full-affirmative`, `:modal-strength`, `:refer-person`, `performance`).\n9\t\n10\t## 1. What a UMR is\n11\t\n12\tA UMR has two levels.\n13\t\n14\t- **Sentence level**: one rooted, directed graph per sentence in PENMAN notation, adapted from AMR:\n15\t  concepts (events, entities, abstract concepts), the relations between them (participant roles and\n16\t  non-participant roles) and attributes (aspect, polarity, mode, quantity, person and number, degree).\n17\t- **Document level**: three kinds of relations between the nodes of the sentence graphs, across sentences:\n18\t  **temporal** dependencies, **modal** dependencies, and **coreference**.\n19\t\n20\t```\n21\tHe denied any wrongdoing.\n22\t\n23\t(s3d / deny-01\n24\t    :ARG0 (s3p / person\n25\t        :refer-person 3rd\n26\t        :refer-number singular)\n27\t    :ARG1 (s3d2 / do-02\n28\t        :ARG0 s3p\n29\t        :ARG1 (s3t / thing\n30\t            :mod (s3a / any)\n31\t            :ARG1-of (s3w / wrong-02))\n32\t        :aspect process)\n33\t    :aspect performance)\n34\t\n35\t(s3s0 / sentence\n36\t    :temporal ((document-creation-time :before s3d)\n37\t               (s3d :before s3d2))\n38\t    :modal ((root :modal author)\n39\t            (author :full-affirmative s3p)\n40\t            (s3p :full-affirmative s3d)\n41\t            (s3d :unspecified s3d2))\n42\t    :coref ((s2p :same-entity s3p)))\n43\t```\n44\t\n45\t**Variables.** Every node of sentence N has a variable starting with `s` + N (`s3d`, `s3p`, `s3d2`), all\n46\tdifferent within the document. A node that fills two roles is written once and then referred to by its\n47\tvariable (re-entrancy: `:ARG0 s3p` above).\n48\t\n49\t## 2. Concepts\n50\t\n51\t### 2.1 Events\n52\t\n53\tAn event is a process or a state that is **predicated**, or a process used to **refer or modify**:\n54\t\n55\t- verbs used as predicates (`deny-01`, Chinese `拥有-01`);\n56\t- event nominals with process meaning (*the meeting*, *the attack*, 会议, 比赛) — annotate them as events;\n57\t- participles and relative-clause verbs (*the man who left*: `(p / person :ARG0-of (l / leave-11))`);\n58\t- predicated states: adjectives and nouns in predicate position (*he is tall*, 他很高) — through the\n59\t  abstract predicates of 2.3 when there is no lexical verb.\n60\t\n61\tNot events: entity nouns, property words used as plain modifiers (*a tall man*: `:mod (t / tall)`),\n62\tauxiliaries and modal verbs (*will, can, must*; 会, 能, 可以, 应该 — they affect `:aspect` and modal\n63\tstrength, they are not nodes), light verbs (*make a decision* → `decide-01`; 进行访问 → `访问-01`),\n64\tthe copula (*be*, 是) — it becomes one of the abstract predicates.\n65\t\n66\t**Concept names.** An English event is its PropBank roleset (`deny-01`, `look-up-05`). A Chinese event is the\n67\tChinese word as written with its Chinese PropBank sense (`拥有-01`, `执行-01`); a Chinese predicate that is not\n68\tin PropBank is `<word>-00`. Entities and modifiers are the word itself, lemmatized in English (`doctor`, `health`) and as\n69\twritten in Chinese (`宇航员`, `航天器`). Use English only for the abstract concepts below and for entity\n70\ttypes (`person`, `thing`, `country`, `city`, `organization`, ...).\n71\t\n72\t### 2.2 Named entities\n73\t\n74\t`(c / country :wiki \"Russia\" :name (n / name :op1 \"Russia\"))`: an entity-type concept, an optional `:wiki`\n75\t(the Wikipedia title, or `-` when there is none), and `:name` with one `:opN` per word of the name.\n76\tChinese names are split into words, not characters: 欧洲议会 → `:op1 \"欧洲\" :op2 \"议会\"`. Types: `person`,\n77\t`country`, `city`, `province`, `state`, `nationality`, `ethnic-group`, `language`, `organization`,\n78\t`company`, `government-organization`, `political-party`, `university`, `newspaper`, `publication`, `river`,\n79\t`mountain`, `building`, `airport`, `road`, `event`, `war`, `conference`, `festival`, `award`, `law`,\n80\t`disease`, `product`, `spaceship`, ... (as in AMR).\n81\t\n82\tDates: `(d / date-entity :year 1998 :month 4 :day 3)`, also `:weekday`, `:season`, `:dayperiod`, `:era`,\n83\t`:decade`, `:century`. Durations and amounts: `(t / temporal-quantity :quant 3 :unit (y / year))`,\n84\t`(m / monetary-quantity :quant 500 :unit (d / dollar))`. Ordinals: `(o / ordinal-entity :value 2)`.\n85\tNumbers are written as digits. In Chinese, a real measure word is a unit (三杯水 → `水 :quant 3 :unit 杯`);\n86\tan ordinary classifier (个, 本, 只, 位) is dropped (三本书 → `书 :quant 3`).\n87\t\n88\t### 2.3 Abstract predicates (non-verbal clauses)\n89\t\n90\t| construction | concept | roles |\n91\t|---|---|---|\n92\t| X is Y (identity, equation) | `identity-91` | `:ARG1` X, `:ARG2` Y |\n93\t| X is Adj / X has property | `have-mod-91` | `:ARG1` theme, `:ARG2` property |\n94\t| X is in/at place | `have-place-91` | `:ARG1` theme, `:ARG2` place |\n95\t| X has Y (possession) | `have-91` | `:ARG1` possessor, `:ARG2` possessum |\n96\t| Y belongs to X | `belong-91` | `:ARG1` possessum, `:ARG2` possessor |\n97\t| there is X (in L) | `exist-91` | `:ARG1` location, `:ARG2` theme |\n98\t| X is a member of category Y | `have-role-91` | `:ARG1` X, `:ARG3` category |\n99\t| X holds title T in organization O | `have-org-role-92` | `:ARG1` X, `:ARG2` O, `:ARG3` T |\n100\t| X is the R (kin, friend) of Z | `have-rel-role-92` | `:ARG1` X, `:ARG2` Z, `:ARG3` R |\n101\t| X has quantity Q | `have-quant-91` | `:ARG1` X, `:ARG2` Q |\n102\t| cause, reason | `have-cause-91` / `:cause` | |\n103\t\n104\tRole nouns are reified the same way: *the president of Russia* →\n105\t`(p / person :ARG1-of (h / have-org-role-92 :ARG2 (c / country ...) :ARG3 (p2 / president)))`;\n106\t*his wife* → `(p / person :ARG1-of (h / have-rel-role-92 :ARG2 p2 :ARG3 (w / wife)))`.\n107\t\n108\t### 2.4 Pronouns and implicit participants\n109\t\n110\tA pronoun is `person` or `thing` with person and number: 他 / *he* → `(p / person :refer-person 3rd\n111\t:refer-number singular)`; 我们 / *we* → `:refer-person 1st :refer-number plural`; 它 / *it* → `thing` with\n112\t3rd singular. A plural noun gets `:refer-number plural`. A Chinese zero subject or object that is clearly\n113\trecoverable may be added as such a node (aligned to no word) and linked by coreference.\n114\t\n115\t### 2.5 Discourse relations (combining clauses)\n116\t\n117\t- coordination: `(a / and :op1 ... :op2 ...)`, `or`;\n118\t- contrast: `(c / contrast-91 :ARG1 ... :ARG2 ...)`; *but* (但是, 却, 然而) `but-91`;\n119\t- concession `:concession`, condition `:condition`, cause `:cause` (或 `have-cause-91`), purpose `:purpose`,\n120\t  manner `:manner`, temporal sequence `:temporal (b / before :op1 ...)` / `after` / `consecutive`;\n121\t- *if* (如果, 只要) → `:condition` on the main event; *because* (因为, 由于) → `:cause`; *in order to* (为了)\n122\t  → `:purpose`; *although* (虽然, 尽管) → `:concession`.\n123\t\n124\t### 2.6 Multi-word expressions\n125\t\n126\tAn idiom, a phrasal verb or a fixed expression is one concept (`look-up-05`, `give-up-07`; a Chinese chengyu\n127\tis one concept). A discontinuous word (帮……忙) is one concept aligned to its span.\n128\t\n129\t## 3. Relations\n130\t\n131\t**Participant roles** (Stage 1): the numbered roles of the predicate's roleset, `:ARG0` (agent, causer,\n132\texperiencer as initiator), `:ARG1` (patient, theme), `:ARG2`–`:ARG5` as the roleset defines them. Use the\n133\tinverse `:ARGn-of` for a relative clause or a participant nominal: 卖菜的人 → `(p / person :ARG0-of (m /\n134\t卖-01 :ARG1 (c / 菜)))`.\n135\t\n136\t**Non-participant roles** (closed list, the frequent ones): `:mod` (property, demonstrative, typifying\n137\tmodifier), `:possessor`, `:part` / `:part-of`, `:topic`, `:temporal` (time expression or event),\n138\t`:place`, `:direction`, `:path`, `:goal`, `:source`, `:start`, `:manner`, `:instrument`, `:cause`,\n139\t`:reason`, `:purpose`, `:condition`, `:concession`, `:duration`, `:frequency`, `:extent`, `:degree`,\n140\t`:quant`, `:unit`, `:ord`, `:range`, `:age`, `:medium`, `:companion`, `:beneficiary`, `:example`,\n141\t`:subset-of`, `:name`, `:wiki`, `:opN`, `:vocative`, `:polite`, `:other-role` (when none fits).\n142\t\n143\tReported speech and thought: the reported event is the `:ARG1` of the reporting verb, as in AMR. The\n144\tUMR release data also marks it with `:quote <reporting event>` when the content is a quotation.\n145\t\n146\t## 4. Attributes\n147\t\n148\t### 4.1 Aspect (`:aspect`, on every event)\n149\t\n150\tUse the coarse values:\n151\t\n152\t| value | use for |\n153\t|---|---|\n154\t| `state` | states: non-verbal predicates (2.3), *be, have, know, want, like, believe*, 是, 有, 在, 知道, 喜欢, 认为 |\n155\t| `habitual` | an event that happens usually or repeatedly (*he walks to work*, 经常, 每天) |\n156\t| `activity` | an ongoing process with no evidence of an end (*is running*, 正在, 着; a present-tense process; most generic statements of what is done) |\n157\t| `endeavor` | a process that ended without reaching its result (*worked on it for a week*, stop doing) |\n158\t| `performance` | a process that ended and reached its result: completed past events (*denied*, *was arrested*, 了, 过) |\n159\t| `process` | an event nominal, or a process whose type cannot be told (*the meeting*, 探索 as a noun) |\n160\t\n161\tFiner values (`inceptive`, `directed-activity`, `semelfactive`, ...) exist in the full guideline; use them\n162\tonly when they are clear.\n163\t\n164\t### 4.2 Polarity, mode, degree, quantity\n165\t\n166\t- Negation: `:polarity -` on the negated concept (没, 不, 未, *not, never*); the event's modal strength is\n167\t  then negative (5.2).\n168\t- `:mode imperative` (commands, 请, 别), `:mode interrogative` (questions; the questioned argument is\n169\t  `amr-unknown`, a yes/no question gets `:polarity amr-unknown`), `:mode expressive` (interjections).\n170\t- `:degree` for intensifiers and downtoners (*very, too, most*; 很, 非常, 太, 最) as a concept:\n171\t  `:degree (v / very)`.\n172\t- `:quant` for numbers and quantifiers (`:quant 3`, `:quant (a / all)`).\n173\t\n174\t## 5. Document level\n175\t\n176\tThe document-level annotation of sentence N is one graph `(sNs0 / sentence ...)` with up to three lists of\n177\t`(parent :relation child)` triples. The triples of sentence N have at least one node of sentence N; the\n178\tother end can be a node of an earlier sentence or a constant.\n179\t\n180\t### 5.1 Temporal (`:temporal`)\n181\t\n182\tA dependency structure: each event and each time expression gets **one reference time** as its parent, and\n183\tthe relation says where the **child** is relative to the **parent**:\n184\t\n185\t- `:before` (child before parent), `:after` (child after parent), `:overlap` (child and parent overlap),\n186\t  `:contained` (child inside parent), `:contains`, `:depends-on` (between time nodes).\n187\t- Constants: `document-creation-time`, `past-reference`, `present-reference`, `future-reference`, `root`.\n188\t- Time expressions first: an absolute date (*1998*) depends on `root`; a relative one (*today, yesterday*,\n189\t  今天) depends on `document-creation-time` — `(document-creation-time :depends-on s1t)`; a vague one\n190\t  (*nowadays*, 现在) on `past-reference` / `present-reference` / `future-reference`.\n191\t- Then every event: (1) a time expression of its clause, if there is one (`(s1t :contained s1e)`); else\n192\t  (2) an event before it that is a clear reference time (`(s2c :before s3a)`); else (3)\n193\t  `document-creation-time` (`(document-creation-time :before s3d)` for a past event, `:overlap` for a\n194\t  present state, `:after` for a future event). Chinese has no tense: decide past, present or future from\n195\t  context, 了, 过, 将, 会.\n196\t- An event in a complement clause takes its matrix event as parent; a purpose is `:after` its main event.\n197\t- Annotate event–event relations only when they are clear.\n198\t\n199\t### 5.2 Modal (`:modal`)\n200\t\n201\tA dependency structure of conceivers and events: `(conceiver :strength event)`, starting with\n202\t`(root :modal author)` in every sentence that has events.\n203\t\n204\t- Strength = the conceiver's certainty that the event happens or holds: `full-affirmative` (plain\n205\t  assertion, *will*), `partial-affirmative` (*probably, must, should*, 应该, 可能性大, 一定 as inference),\n206\t  `neutral-affirmative` (*may, might, perhaps, can*, 可能, 也许, 或许, desire/hope), `neutral-negative`,\n207\t  `partial-negative`, `full-negative` (negated: *not, never, deny*, 没, 不), `unspecified`.\n208\t- Every event the author asserts: `(author :full-affirmative s1e)`.\n209\t- Reported speech or thought: the author is certain of the speaker, the speaker of the content:\n210\t  `(author :full-affirmative s9p)` `(s9p :full-affirmative s9s3)`, where `s9p` is the speaker (a person\n211\t  node, or an event used as conceiver).\n212\t- An event under a modal or complement-taking predicate (*want, try, plan, decide, hope*, 想, 希望, 打算,\n213\t  决定, 需要): the predicate is asserted by the author, the complement has the predicate as parent with\n214\t  `unspecified` (or the strength the predicate implies): `(author :full-affirmative s3w)`\n215\t  `(s3w :unspecified s3g)`.\n216\t- A conceiver that is not in the text is `null-conceiver`. Purpose and condition clauses may use the\n217\t  constants `purpose` and `have-condition` as parents of their events.\n218\t- In the sentence graph, write the strength of an event that is not `full-affirmative` from the author as\n219\t  `:modal-strength <value>` too (the scorers move it to the document level).\n220\t\n221\t### 5.3 Coreference (`:coref`)\n222\t\n223\t- `(earlier :same-entity later)`: two mentions of the same entity — a pronoun and its antecedent, a repeated\n224\t  name, a definite description. Link each mention to its closest earlier mention, earlier one first;\n225\t  link entity nodes, never `name` nodes.\n226\t- `(earlier :same-event later)`: two mentions of the same event.\n227\t- `(a :subset-of b)`: the entity or event a is a subset or part of b.\n228\t- Within one sentence, coreference is a re-entrancy in the graph (one variable), not a `:coref` triple.\n229\t\n230\t## 6. Alignment\n231\t\n232\tEvery node of a sentence graph is aligned to the words that express it: `s1x: 4-4` (1-based word positions,\n233\tfirst and last, both included). A node with no word in the sentence (`person` for a zero pronoun, `and`\n234\twithout a conjunction, `have-mod-91` without a copula, a `name` node) is `0-0`. A named entity's type\n235\tnode is aligned to the name's words.\n236\t\n237\t## 7. Checklist\n238\t\n239\t- Parentheses balanced; every variable defined once; all variables start with `s<N>`.\n240\t- Every event has `:aspect`; negated events have `:polarity -`.\n241\t- Every event and time expression has a temporal parent; every event has a modal parent.\n242\t- Coreference across sentences is in `:coref`; within a sentence it is a re-entrancy.\n243\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3650b4e1-0dc2-4628-8fb8-e7afc538b1a1","uuid":"eafce9e2-c7a7-4064-8b8f-e0695ae1bd9e","timestamp":"2026-11-10T07:22:46.208Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/umr-harness/s2-gold/zh-0032/guidelines/umr_guidelines_abridged.md","content":"# UMR guidelines, abridged\n\nA short version of the UMR 0.9 specification (`umr_guidelines.md` in this folder, umr4nlp/umr-guidelines),\nfor annotating Chinese and English at Stage 1 (numbered PropBank roles). It keeps what an annotator needs for\nmost sentences and leaves out the typological material, the fine-grained lattices, the field-linguistics\nsections and most of the examples. Where the full guideline uses the older short names (`AUTH`, `DCT`,\n`FullAff`, `:modstr`, `:ref-person`, `Performance`), this version uses the names of the UMR release data\n(`author`, `document-creation-time`, `full-affirmative`, `:modal-strength`, `:refer-person`, `performance`).\n\n## 1. What a UMR is\n\nA UMR has two levels.\n\n- **Sentence level**: one rooted, directed graph per sentence in PENMAN notation, adapted from AMR:\n  concepts (events, entities, abstract concepts), the relations between them (participant roles and\n  non-participant roles) and attributes (aspect, polarity, mode, quantity, person and number, degree).\n- **Document level**: three kinds of relations between the nodes of the sentence graphs, across sentences:\n  **temporal** dependencies, **modal** dependencies, and **coreference**.\n\n```\nHe denied any wrongdoing.\n\n(s3d / deny-01\n    :ARG0 (s3p / person\n        :refer-person 3rd\n        :refer-number singular)\n    :ARG1 (s3d2 / do-02\n        :ARG0 s3p\n        :ARG1 (s3t / thing\n            :mod (s3a / any)\n            :ARG1-of (s3w / wrong-02))\n        :aspect process)\n    :aspect performance)\n\n(s3s0 / sentence\n    :temporal ((document-creation-time :before s3d)\n               (s3d :before s3d2))\n    :modal ((root :modal author)\n            (author :full-affirmative s3p)\n            (s3p :full-affirmative s3d)\n            (s3d :unspecified s3d2))\n    :coref ((s2p :same-entity s3p)))\n```\n\n**Variables.** Every node of sentence N has a variable starting with `s` + N (`s3d`, `s3p`, `s3d2`), all\ndifferent within the document. A node that fills two roles is written once and then referred to by its\nvariable (re-entrancy: `:ARG0 s3p` above).\n\n## 2. Concepts\n\n### 2.1 Events\n\nAn event is a process or a state that is **predicated**, or a process used to **refer or modify**:\n\n- verbs used as predicates (`deny-01`, Chinese `拥有-01`);\n- event nominals with process meaning (*the meeting*, *the attack*, 会议, 比赛) — annotate them as events;\n- participles and relative-clause verbs (*the man who left*: `(p / person :ARG0-of (l / leave-11))`);\n- predicated states: adjectives and nouns in predicate position (*he is tall*, 他很高) — through the\n  abstract predicates of 2.3 when there is no lexical verb.\n\nNot events: entity nouns, property words used as plain modifiers (*a tall man*: `:mod (t / tall)`),\nauxiliaries and modal verbs (*will, can, must*; 会, 能, 可以, 应该 — they affect `:aspect` and modal\nstrength, they are not nodes), light verbs (*make a decision* → `decide-01`; 进行访问 → `访问-01`),\nthe copula (*be*, 是) — it becomes one of the abstract predicates.\n\n**Concept names.** An English event is its PropBank roleset (`deny-01`, `look-up-05`). A Chinese event is the\nChinese word as written with its Chinese PropBank sense (`拥有-01`, `执行-01`); a Chinese predicate that is not\nin PropBank is `<word>-00`. Entities and modifiers are the word itself, lemmatized in English (`doctor`, `health`) and as\nwritten in Chinese (`宇航员`, `航天器`). Use English only for the abstract concepts below and for entity\ntypes (`person`, `thing`, `country`, `city`, `organization`, ...).\n\n### 2.2 Named entities\n\n`(c / country :wiki \"Russia\" :name (n / name :op1 \"Russia\"))`: an entity-type concept, an optional `:wiki`\n(the Wikipedia title, or `-` when there is none), and `:name` with one `:opN` per word of the name.\nChinese names are split into words, not characters: 欧洲议会 → `:op1 \"欧洲\" :op2 \"议会\"`. Types: `person`,\n`country`, `city`, `province`, `state`, `nationality`, `ethnic-group`, `language`, `organization`,\n`company`, `government-organization`, `political-party`, `university`, `newspaper`, `publication`, `river`,\n`mountain`, `building`, `airport`, `road`, `event`, `war`, `conference`, `festival`, `award`, `law`,\n`disease`, `product`, `spaceship`, ... (as in AMR).\n\nDates: `(d / date-entity :year 1998 :month 4 :day 3)`, also `:weekday`, `:season`, `:dayperiod`, `:era`,\n`:decade`, `:century`. Durations and amounts: `(t / temporal-quantity :quant 3 :unit (y / year))`,\n`(m / monetary-quantity :quant 500 :unit (d / dollar))`. Ordinals: `(o / ordinal-entity :value 2)`.\nNumbers are written as digits. In Chinese, a real measure word is a unit (三杯水 → `水 :quant 3 :unit 杯`);\nan ordinary classifier (个, 本, 只, 位) is dropped (三本书 → `书 :quant 3`).\n\n### 2.3 Abstract predicates (non-verbal clauses)\n\n| construction | concept | roles |\n|---|---|---|\n| X is Y (identity, equation) | `identity-91` | `:ARG1` X, `:ARG2` Y |\n| X is Adj / X has property | `have-mod-91` | `:ARG1` theme, `:ARG2` property |\n| X is in/at place | `have-place-91` | `:ARG1` theme, `:ARG2` place |\n| X has Y (possession) | `have-91` | `:ARG1` possessor, `:ARG2` possessum |\n| Y belongs to X | `belong-91` | `:ARG1` possessum, `:ARG2` possessor |\n| there is X (in L) | `exist-91` | `:ARG1` location, `:ARG2` theme |\n| X is a member of category Y | `have-role-91` | `:ARG1` X, `:ARG3` category |\n| X holds title T in organization O | `have-org-role-92` | `:ARG1` X, `:ARG2` O, `:ARG3` T |\n| X is the R (kin, friend) of Z | `have-rel-role-92` | `:ARG1` X, `:ARG2` Z, `:ARG3` R |\n| X has quantity Q | `have-quant-91` | `:ARG1` X, `:ARG2` Q |\n| cause, reason | `have-cause-91` / `:cause` | |\n\nRole nouns are reified the same way: *the president of Russia* →\n`(p / person :ARG1-of (h / have-org-role-92 :ARG2 (c / country ...) :ARG3 (p2 / president)))`;\n*his wife* → `(p / person :ARG1-of (h / have-rel-role-92 :ARG2 p2 :ARG3 (w / wife)))`.\n\n### 2.4 Pronouns and implicit participants\n\nA pronoun is `person` or `thing` with person and number: 他 / *he* → `(p / person :refer-person 3rd\n:refer-number singular)`; 我们 / *we* → `:refer-person 1st :refer-number plural`; 它 / *it* → `thing` with\n3rd singular. A plural noun gets `:refer-number plural`. A Chinese zero subject or object that is clearly\nrecoverable may be added as such a node (aligned to no word) and linked by coreference.\n\n### 2.5 Discourse relations (combining clauses)\n\n- coordination: `(a / and :op1 ... :op2 ...)`, `or`;\n- contrast: `(c / contrast-91 :ARG1 ... :ARG2 ...)`; *but* (但是, 却, 然而) `but-91`;\n- concession `:concession`, condition `:condition`, cause `:cause` (或 `have-cause-91`), purpose `:purpose`,\n  manner `:manner`, temporal sequence `:temporal (b / before :op1 ...)` / `after` / `consecutive`;\n- *if* (如果, 只要) → `:condition` on the main event; *because* (因为, 由于) → `:cause`; *in order to* (为了)\n  → `:purpose`; *although* (虽然, 尽管) → `:concession`.\n\n### 2.6 Multi-word expressions\n\nAn idiom, a phrasal verb or a fixed expression is one concept (`look-up-05`, `give-up-07`; a Chinese chengyu\nis one concept). A discontinuous word (帮……忙) is one concept aligned to its span.\n\n## 3. Relations\n\n**Participant roles** (Stage 1): the numbered roles of the predicate's roleset, `:ARG0` (agent, causer,\nexperiencer as initiator), `:ARG1` (patient, theme), `:ARG2`–`:ARG5` as the roleset defines them. Use the\ninverse `:ARGn-of` for a relative clause or a participant nominal: 卖菜的人 → `(p / person :ARG0-of (m /\n卖-01 :ARG1 (c / 菜)))`.\n\n**Non-participant roles** (closed list, the frequent ones): `:mod` (property, demonstrative, typifying\nmodifier), `:possessor`, `:part` / `:part-of`, `:topic`, `:temporal` (time expression or event),\n`:place`, `:direction`, `:path`, `:goal`, `:source`, `:start`, `:manner`, `:instrument`, `:cause`,\n`:reason`, `:purpose`, `:condition`, `:concession`, `:duration`, `:frequency`, `:extent`, `:degree`,\n`:quant`, `:unit`, `:ord`, `:range`, `:age`, `:medium`, `:companion`, `:beneficiary`, `:example`,\n`:subset-of`, `:name`, `:wiki`, `:opN`, `:vocative`, `:polite`, `:other-role` (when none fits).\n\nReported speech and thought: the reported event is the `:ARG1` of the reporting verb, as in AMR. The\nUMR release data also marks it with `:quote <reporting event>` when the content is a quotation.\n\n## 4. Attributes\n\n### 4.1 Aspect (`:aspect`, on every event)\n\nUse the coarse values:\n\n| value | use for |\n|---|---|\n| `state` | states: non-verbal predicates (2.3), *be, have, know, want, like, believe*, 是, 有, 在, 知道, 喜欢, 认为 |\n| `habitual` | an event that happens usually or repeatedly (*he walks to work*, 经常, 每天) |\n| `activity` | an ongoing process with no evidence of an end (*is running*, 正在, 着; a present-tense process; most generic statements of what is done) |\n| `endeavor` | a process that ended without reaching its result (*worked on it for a week*, stop doing) |\n| `performance` | a process that ended and reached its result: completed past events (*denied*, *was arrested*, 了, 过) |\n| `process` | an event nominal, or a process whose type cannot be told (*the meeting*, 探索 as a noun) |\n\nFiner values (`inceptive`, `directed-activity`, `semelfactive`, ...) exist in the full guideline; use them\nonly when they are clear.\n\n### 4.2 Polarity, mode, degree, quantity\n\n- Negation: `:polarity -` on the negated concept (没, 不, 未, *not, never*); the event's modal strength is\n  then negative (5.2).\n- `:mode imperative` (commands, 请, 别), `:mode interrogative` (questions; the questioned argument is\n  `amr-unknown`, a yes/no question gets `:polarity amr-unknown`), `:mode expressive` (interjections).\n- `:degree` for intensifiers and downtoners (*very, too, most*; 很, 非常, 太, 最) as a concept:\n  `:degree (v / very)`.\n- `:quant` for numbers and quantifiers (`:quant 3`, `:quant (a / all)`).\n\n## 5. Document level\n\nThe document-level annotation of sentence N is one graph `(sNs0 / sentence ...)` with up to three lists of\n`(parent :relation child)` triples. The triples of sentence N have at least one node of sentence N; the\nother end can be a node of an earlier sentence or a constant.\n\n### 5.1 Temporal (`:temporal`)\n\nA dependency structure: each event and each time expression gets **one reference time** as its parent, and\nthe relation says where the **child** is relative to the **parent**:\n\n- `:before` (child before parent), `:after` (child after parent), `:overlap` (child and parent overlap),\n  `:contained` (child inside parent), `:contains`, `:depends-on` (between time nodes).\n- Constants: `document-creation-time`, `past-reference`, `present-reference`, `future-reference`, `root`.\n- Time expressions first: an absolute date (*1998*) depends on `root`; a relative one (*today, yesterday*,\n  今天) depends on `document-creation-time` — `(document-creation-time :depends-on s1t)`; a vague one\n  (*nowadays*, 现在) on `past-reference` / `present-reference` / `future-reference`.\n- Then every event: (1) a time expression of its clause, if there is one (`(s1t :contained s1e)`); else\n  (2) an event before it that is a clear reference time (`(s2c :before s3a)`); else (3)\n  `document-creation-time` (`(document-creation-time :before s3d)` for a past event, `:overlap` for a\n  present state, `:after` for a future event). Chinese has no tense: decide past, present or future from\n  context, 了, 过, 将, 会.\n- An event in a complement clause takes its matrix event as parent; a purpose is `:after` its main event.\n- Annotate event–event relations only when they are clear.\n\n### 5.2 Modal (`:modal`)\n\nA dependency structure of conceivers and events: `(conceiver :strength event)`, starting with\n`(root :modal author)` in every sentence that has events.\n\n- Strength = the conceiver's certainty that the event happens or holds: `full-affirmative` (plain\n  assertion, *will*), `partial-affirmative` (*probably, must, should*, 应该, 可能性大, 一定 as inference),\n  `neutral-affirmative` (*may, might, perhaps, can*, 可能, 也许, 或许, desire/hope), `neutral-negative`,\n  `partial-negative`, `full-negative` (negated: *not, never, deny*, 没, 不), `unspecified`.\n- Every event the author asserts: `(author :full-affirmative s1e)`.\n- Reported speech or thought: the author is certain of the speaker, the speaker of the content:\n  `(author :full-affirmative s9p)` `(s9p :full-affirmative s9s3)`, where `s9p` is the speaker (a person\n  node, or an event used as conceiver).\n- An event under a modal or complement-taking predicate (*want, try, plan, decide, hope*, 想, 希望, 打算,\n  决定, 需要): the predicate is asserted by the author, the complement has the predicate as parent with\n  `unspecified` (or the strength the predicate implies): `(author :full-affirmative s3w)`\n  `(s3w :unspecified s3g)`.\n- A conceiver that is not in the text is `null-conceiver`. Purpose and condition clauses may use the\n  constants `purpose` and `have-condition` as parents of their events.\n- In the sentence graph, write the strength of an event that is not `full-affirmative` from the author as\n  `:modal-strength <value>` too (the scorers move it to the document level).\n\n### 5.3 Coreference (`:coref`)\n\n- `(earlier :same-entity later)`: two mentions of the same entity — a pronoun and its antecedent, a repeated\n  name, a definite description. Link each mention to its closest earlier mention, earlier one first;\n  link entity nodes, never `name` nodes.\n- `(earlier :same-event later)`: two mentions of the same event.\n- `(a :subset-of b)`: the entity or event a is a subset or part of b.\n- Within one sentence, coreference is a re-entrancy in the graph (one variable), not a `:coref` triple.\n\n## 6. Alignment\n\nEvery node of a sentence graph is aligned to the words that express it: `s1x: 4-4` (1-based word positions,\nfirst and last, both included). A node with no word in the sentence (`person` for a zero pronoun, `and`\nwithout a conjunction, `have-mod-91` without a copula, a `name` node) is `0-0`. A named entity's type\nnode is aligned to the name's words.\n\n## 7. Checklist\n\n- Parentheses balanced; every variable defined once; all variables start with `s<N>`.\n- Every event has `:aspect`; negated events have `:polarity -`.\n- Every event and time expression has a temporal parent; every event has a modal parent.\n- Coreference across sentences is in `:coref`; within a sentence it is a re-entrancy.\n","numLines":243,"startLine":1,"totalLines":243}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01XfXjSHJbzPNf4bkQ67pCpa","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
